            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance news is that NASA’s rover captured a first-of-its-kind view from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The image sequence was taken by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026, around 7 p.m. local Mars time, and NASA/JPL published the news on Aug. 5, 2026. NASA says this is the first time humanity has captured, from the surface of another planet, Earth being occulted by another object【6:0†source】.\n\nA recent science update from July 15, 2026, is also notable: Perseverance found evidence that a 245-foot-thick, or 75-meter-thick, stack of ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater was formed by repeated asteroid impacts. The area, called the “Broom Point member,” is likely more than 3.9 billion years old and may preserve a rare record from an early, violent era of solar-system history【6:3†source】.\n\nIn short: the newest headline is a striking Earth–Phobos occultation image, while the latest major geology result is Perseverance’s investigation of ancient impact-built rocks on Jezero Crater’s rim.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-watches-earth-vanish-behind-martian-moon/",
